WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.549 --> 00:00:04.509
It's taken a few years, but we've finally
gotten some clarification on perhaps the most

00:00:04.509 --> 00:00:07.200
messed-up moment in the ​Star Wars​ prequel
trilogy.

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:11.360
The moment in question comes in Episode II:
Attack of the Clones when it appears that

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.599
young Boba Fett is grieving over the death
of his father, Jango while seemingly holding

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:16.540
his severed head.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:21.019
Actor Daniel Logan, who portrayed Boba in
the film, recently took to Twitter to state

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.160
that this was not exactly as it seemed.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:27.900
You may remember that near the film's end,
Anakin and Padme are captured by Jango as

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.780
they're attempting to reach Obi-Wan Kenobi.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:34.859
Luckily, a group of Jedi arrive to rescue
them, including Yoda and Mace Windu, who takes

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:35.660
on Jango.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.390
The Master Jedi proves to be too formidable
for the bounty hunter, who is quickly decapitated

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:41.040
by Windu.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:45.420
As the fighting continues, a brief shot shows
young Boba apparently picking up the helmet

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:49.760
containing his father's severed head, looking
appropriately traumatized.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.809
The beheading and Boba's reaction stands as
one of the most disturbing happenings in Star

00:00:53.809 --> 00:00:59.210
Wars history; sure, Jango is a ruthless mercenary
and Boba is a clone, but the sight of a kid

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:04.059
clutching his parental figure's decapitated
head seems just a little bit intense for the

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:04.560
series.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:08.960
However, according to Logan, when Boba picks
up that helmet, dear old dad's head is not

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:09.970
actually in it.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:14.100
"You can see the shadow of Jango's head falling
out just after Mace 'removes' it from his

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.700
body and the helmet goes flying.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.660
So, no, no head in it when #BobaFett picks
up the helmet.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.540
Thanks @SamuelLJackson!"

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:24.920
We're not sure why Logan would deign to offer
thanks to the dude who lightsabered his dad's

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:25.420
head off.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.140
At any rate, it's telling that despite the
answer to the question being right there in

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:34.030
the film, the shadowy image of Jango's falling
head slipped by so many fans that Logan had

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:35.130
to set the record straight.

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:39.220
Perhaps, even in shadow, the image was just
too gruesome for director George Lucas to

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:40.920
linger on for too terribly long.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.670
"Unlikely but seems to be true!"

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.960
Of course, it's not like the Star Wars movies
don't have their share of dark moments; even

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:51.680
the original trilogy hit us with scenes like
the aftermath of the stormtrooper raid on

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:54.860
the home of Luke's aunt and uncle in Episode
IV: A New Hope.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.950
If that's not enough for you, there's always
Darth Vader performing an impromptu amputation

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.780
of Luke's hand in Episode V: The Empire Strikes
Back.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:06.230
For the really bloodthirsty, there's also
Luke's merciless torture at the hands of Palpatine

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:10.360
while screaming for his help at the conclusion
of Episode VI: Return of the Jedi.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:15.220
Still, don't forget that Disney has stewardship
of the entire Star Wars series, having purchased

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:16.860
Lucasfilm in 2012.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:22.640
A bloodless onscreen decapitation is one thing;
a small child holding his father's head is

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:23.420
quite another.

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:27.260
What we're saying is that if it weren't made
clear within the film that the helmet was

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:31.480
empty when Boba picks it up, Disney may have
had to go back to do a little Lucas-style

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:32.820
revision to make it so.

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:37.660
Of course, when you think about it, this raises
another question: for the remainder of the

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:40.020
battle, where exactly was Jango's head?

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.340
Was it just kind of sitting there on the ground,
and if so, was it somewhere that young Boba

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:45.000
could see it?

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:49.380
Upon further reflection, we're thinking that
Jango's head probably was hanging around for

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:54.040
the rest of the battle, which means that Logan's
explanation actually serves to make the tragic

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.320
scene even worse.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.020
Well, nobody ever said that being the cloned
son of a Mandalorian mercenary in a galaxy

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:01.480
far, far away was easy.

